# Prevent Svelte Warning Regressions

When editing Svelte files, avoid patterns that trigger recurring `svelte-check` warnings.

## 1) Avoid `state_referenced_locally` with page `data`

- Do not initialize `$state(...)` directly from `data.*` values from `$props()`.
- For read-only derived values, use `$derived(...)`.
- For editable form state, initialize with literals and sync from `data` in `$effect(...)`.

```svelte
<!-- ❌ BAD -->
let { data } = $props();
let search = $state(data.filters.search);

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
let { data } = $props();
let search = $state('');
$effect(() => { search = data.filters.search; });
```

## 2) Never nest invalid interactive/form elements

- Never put `<button>` inside another `<button>`.
- Never put `<form>` inside another `<form>`.
- For per-action submits inside a main form, use submit buttons with `formaction`/`formmethod`.

```svelte
<!-- ✅ preferred inside an existing form -->
<button type="submit" formaction="?/toggleThing" formmethod="POST" name="id" value={item.id}>
  Toggle
</button>
```

## 3) Ensure every `<label>` is associated to a control

- Use `for="..."` + matching control `id="..."`, or wrap the control inside `<label>`.
- If text is only a visual section heading (not a label), use `<div>`/`<span>` instead of `<label>`.

```svelte
<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
<label for="seasonId">Season</label>
<select id="seasonId" name="seasonId"></select>
```

Before finishing Svelte/UI work, run `bun run check` and address new warnings in touched files.
